 to use the given conversion factor between kJ mol⁻¹ and cm⁻¹, it\'s likely that we can work entirely in cm⁻¹.\n\nThe key formula for entropy-driven spin transitions (from Thompson) is:\n\nλ* = γ e⁻𝜏\n\nΔH = λ G\'\n\nBut the problem says to consider the spin-degeneracy entropy, so we can use:\n\nln(gHS/gLS) = -ΔH / G\' \n\nHowever, the problem states that "neglect all contributions to the spin-state free energy except (i) the ligand-field stabilization and intra-orbital pairing energies, and (ii) the electronic spin-degeneracy entropy."\n\nThis suggests that the total free energy difference is:\n\nG = ΔH - T ΔS \n\nWhere ΔS is the entropy difference, which is positive because S(LS) > S(HS) (LS has higher degeneracy).\n\nWait, S(LS) = (5/2) ln(6) + (1/2) ln(4) = (5/2)(2.3026) + (1/2)(1.3863) = 5.7565 + 0.69315 = 6.44965 \n\nS(HS) = (5/2) ln(6) - (1/2) ln(4) = 5.7565 - 0.69315 = 5.06335 \n\nTherefore,\n\nΔS = S(LS) - S(HS) = 6.44965 - 5.06335 = 1.3863 (approx)\n\nWhich is ln(4) = ln(4!) / 24 = 3 / 24 = 0.125, but wait, no:\n\nWait, ln(6!) = ln(720) = 6.579, ln(4!) = ln(24) = 3.178\n\nS(LS) = (5/2)(6.579) + (1/2)(3.178) = 16.4475 + 1.589 = 18.0365\n\nS(HS) = (5/2)(6.579) - (1/2)(3.178) = 16.4475 - 1.589 = 14.8585\n\nΔS = S(LS) - S(HS) = 18.0365 - 14.8585 = 3.178 \n\nWait, no, that\'s not right.\n\nln(6!) = ln(720) ≈ 6.579\n\nln(4!) = ln(24) ≈ 3.178\n\nSo:\n\nS(LS) = (5/2) * 6.579 + (1/2) * 3.178 = 16.4475 + 1.589 = 18.0365\n\nS(HS) = (5/2) * 6.579 - (1/2) * 3.178 = 16.4475 - 1.589 = 14.8585\n\nTherefore,\n\nln(gHS/gLS) = - (S(HS) - S(LS)) / ln(6) = ln(4!) / ln(6)\n\nBut let\'s not use the standard formula. Instead, use the problem statement\'s instruction to consider entropy.\n\nThe condition for equal population is:\n\nG(LS) = G(HS)\n\nWhich implies:\n\nH(LS) - H(HS) = T (S(LS) - S(HS))\n\nH(LS) - H(HS) = [6 eσ - 4 eπ] - [6 eσ - 4 eπ] + U - IE = U - IE\n\nS(LS) - S(HS) = -S(LS) + S(HS) = - ( (5/2) ln(6!) + (1/2) ln(4!) ) + (5/2) ln(6!) - (1/2) ln(4!) ) \n\nWait, no:\n\nS(LS) - S(HS) = [ (5/2) ln(6!) + (1/2) ln(4!) ) - (5/2) ln(6!) + (1/2) ln(4!) ) = ln(4!) \n\nWait, let\'s write it out:\n\nS(LS) = (5/2) ln(6!) + (1/2) ln(4!)\n\nS(HS) = (5/2) ln(6!) - (1/2) ln(4!)\n\nTherefore,\n\nS(LS) - S(HS) = (5/2) ln(6!) + (1/2) ln(4!) - (5/2) ln(6!) + (1/2) ln(4!) = ln(4!) \n\nYes! Because the (5/2) ln(6!) terms cancel.\n\nSo,\n\nS(LS) - S(HS) = ln(4!) \n\nWhich is a constant, but that can\'t be right. Wait, no:\n\nWait, the enthalpy for LS is H(LS) = 6 eσ - 4 eπ - U/2 (since U = ½ngppN, so npp = U/(½ngp) * N)\n\nFor HS, H(HS) = 6 eσ - 4 eπ - IE\n\nTherefore,\n\nH(LS) - H(HS) = (U/2 - IE)\n\nAnd\n\nS(LS) - S(HS) = ? \n\nWait, no:\n\nThe partition function per spin in LS: gLS = (5/2)(6) + (1/2)(4) = 15 + 2 = 17\n\nFor HS, gHS = (5/2) - (1/2)(4) = 2.5 - 2 = 0.5\n\nTherefore,\n\nln(gHS/gLS) = ln(0.5/17) = ln(1/34) ≈ -3.528\n\nThe entropy difference is related to ln(gHS/gLS) = -ΔS/ Slack (not sure)\n\nBut the problem says "electronic spin-degeneracy entropy", so the entropy contribution to the free energy is T times the change in entropy when going from HS to LS.\n\nSo,\n\nΔG = G(LS) - G(HS) = (H(LS) - H(HS)) - T (S(LS) - S(HS))\n\nGiven that H(LS) - H(HS) = U - IE (since H(LS) = ΔEy - P/2 and H(HS) = ΔEy - IE, so H(LS) - H(HS) = P/2 - IE)\n\nWait, yes:\n\nH(LS) = ΔEy - P/2 = 6 eσ - 4 eπ - 1/2 P\n\nH(HS) = ΔEy - IE = 6 eσ - 4 eπ - IE\n\nThus,\n\nH(LS) - H(HS) = P/2 - IE\n\nTherefore,\n\nΔG = (P/2 - IE) - T (S(LS) - S(HS))\n\nSetting ΔG = 0:\n\nP/2 - IE = T (S(LS) - S(HS))\n\nNow, compute S(LS) - S(HS):\n\nS(LS) = (5/2) ln(6!) + (1/2) ln(4!) \n\nS(HS) = (5/2) ln(6!) - (1/2) ln(4!) \n\nTherefore,\n\nS(LS) - S(HS) = (5/2) ln(6!) + (1/2) ln(4!) - (5/2) ln(6!) + (1/2) ln(4!) = ln(4!) \n\nWait, no:\n\nWait, 6! = 720, 4! = 24\n\nSo,\n\nS(LS) = (5/2) ln(720) + (1/2) ln(24) \n\nS(HS) = (5/2) ln(720) - (1/2) ln(24) \n\nThus,\n\nS(LS) - S(HS) = (5/2) ln(720) + (1/2) ln(24) - (5/2) ln(720) + (1/2) ln(24) = ln(24) \n\nYes, exactly.\n\nln(24) = ln(4! ) = 3.17808\n\nSo,\n\nΔS = S(LS) - S(HS) = ln(4! ) = 3.17808\n\nNow compute P/2 - IE:\n\nP = 15000 cm⁻¹\n\nSo,\n\nP/2 = 7500 cm⁻¹\n\nIE = 6 d0 - 4 d1 - 4π\' \n\nWait, IE = 6 ε - 4 π + 5 π\' + 4 π\'\' \n\nBut in the problem statement, it says "intra-orbital pairing energies", which for HS is equal to 4π (since it\'s one unpaired electron), so IE = 2*(pairing energy for four electrons) = 2*(2π) = 4π (since 4 electrons involved in pairing, 2 pairs).\n\nActually, the correct IE for octahedral configuration t2g⁴ eg² is:\n\nIE = 6 d0 - 4 d1 - 4 π + 5 π\' + 4 π\'\' \n\nBut the problem says "neglect all contributions to the spin-state free energy except (i) the ligand-field stabilization and intra-orbital pairing energies, and (ii) the electronic spin-degeneracy entropy."\n\n"Intra-orbital pairing energies" would be the pairing energy within the t2g and eg orbitals.\n\nFor HS (t2g⁴ eg²), the unpaired electrons are 4 (from t2g: one pair, three unpaired; eg: two unpaired).\n\nWait, no:\n\nS = 2! + 2! = 4 (total unpaired electrons)\n\nPairing energy: In a high-spin d⁶ complex, the configuration is t2g⁴ eg², which has one pair in t2g and two unpaired in t2g, plus two unpaired in eg. Total unpaired electrons = 4.\n\nThe pairing energy is the energy due to creating a pair, which for d⁶ HS is 2 (one pair).\n\nFor LS (t2g⁶ eg⁰), there are 6 electrons, 3 pairs, so pairing energy = 6.\n\nBut the problem states: "intra-orbital pairing energies", which probably refers to the net pairing energy for each state.\n\nFor HS: Unpaired electrons = 4, so bonding orbital occupation involves 4 unpaired electrons, meaning 3 bonding and 1 antibonding. Pairing energy = 2 (1 pair)\n\nFor LS: All t2g filled, 6 electrons, 3 pairs, pairing energy = 6\n\nBut the problem says "intra-orbital", so maybe it\'s the total pairing energy required to put all electrons in, minus the bonding orbitals.\n\nFor bonding orbitals (d0): No electrons, so no energy cost.\n\nFor antibonding orbitals (d1): One electron, cost 2.\n\nFor t2g (d2 to d6): Each t2g orbital holds 2 electrons, so pairing energy increases by 2 per pair formed.\n\nFor eg (d5 to d10): Each eg orbital holds 2 electrons.\n\nIn HS (t2g⁴ eg²):\n\n- t2g has 4 electrons: 2 pairs, so pairing energy cost = 2\n- eg has 2 electrons: 1 pair, cost = 2\n- Total pairing energy = 4? \n\nWait, no:\n\nThe total pairing energy is the sum of the energy penalties for each pair beyond the unpaired electrons.\n\nNumber of unpaired electrons in HS: 4 (t2g: 2 unpaired, eg: 2 unpaired)\n\nNumber of electrons: 6\n\nNumber of paired electrons: 6 - 4 = 2\n\nEach pair costs P (pairing energy), so total pairing energy = 2P (if each pair is a pair of P).\n\nBut in complexes, the pairing energy is usually defined as the energy difference between a doubly occupied orbital and two singly occupied orbitals, so one pair = P.\n\nThus, for HS (4 unpaired), there are 3 doubly occupied orbitals (t2g has 3 orbitals, 2 are doubly occupied; eg has 2 orbitals, 1 is doubly occupied). Total doubly occupied orbitals = 3 + 1 = 4, but wait:\n\nWait, t2g⁴: t2g has three orbitals, 4 electrons, so two orbitals are doubly occupied, one is singly. That\'s 2 pairs.\n\neg²: eg has two orbitals, 2 electrons, so one is doubly occupied, one is singly. That\'s 1 pair.\n\nTotal pairs = 2 + 1 = 3? No, the number of pairs is equal to the number of doubly occupied orbitals.\n\nHS (t2g⁴ eg²):\n\n- t2g: 3 orbitals, 4 electrons → 2 doubly occupied, 1 unprimed orbital. So 2 pairs.\n\n- eg: 2 orbitals, 2 electrons → 1 doubly occupied, 1 unprimed orbital. So 1 pair.\n\nTotal: 3 pairs.\n\nBut each pair costs P, so total pairing energy = 3P. However, this contradicts the standard pairing energy for HS d⁶, which is often considered as 2P (since the free ion has 6 unpaired electrons, so in the complex, you lose 2 pairing energy).\n\nAh, here\'s the correct way:\n\nThe free ion has d⁶ configurat
