[36m(RolloutManager pid=8567)[0m [2026-07-15 13:58:21] inference_rollout_train.py:179 - Finish rollout: ['<s><|im_start|>user\nConsider a nonrelativistic heavy quark of mass M produced at midrapidity at proper time τ0 in a boost-invariant, transversely homogeneous quark-gluon plasma (QGP). The local rest-frame temperature of the QGP evolves with Bjorken scaling as T(τ) = T0 (τ0/τ)^{1/3} for τ ≥ τ0, with T0 > 0. In the local rest frame, the heavy quark momentum p⃗(τ) obeys an Itô Langevin equation with isotropic drag and momentum diffusion:\ndp_i(τ) = -μ(τ) p_i(τ) dτ + √(κ(τ)) dW_i(τ), for i ∈ {x, y, z},\nwith independent Wiener processes W_i such that 〈dW_i(τ)〉 = 0 and 〈dW_i(τ) dW_j(τ)〉 = δ_ij dτ. The drag coefficient is μ(τ) = a T^2(τ) with a > 0 a constant, and the momentum diffusion coefficient obeys the fluctuation-dissipation relation κ(τ) = 2 M T(τ) μ(τ). Work in natural units (ħ = c = k_B = 1).\n\nAssume the heavy quark is created with initial momentum p⃗(τ0) = (p0, 0, 0), i.e., P(p⃗, τ0) = δ(p_x - p0) δ(p_y) δ(p_z). Derive the exact probability density P(p⃗, τ_f) for any τ_f > τ0, and give closed-form expressions (in terms of standard special functions, if necessary) for:\n1) The mean and variance of p_x(τ_f).\n2) The full 3D probability density P(p⃗, τ_f).\n3) The average total kinetic energy 〈E_kin(τ_f)〉 = 〈p^2(τ_f)〉/(2M).\n\nAll variables and parameters are as defined above. The exponential integral Ei(x) is defined by d/dx Ei(x) = e^{x}/x with Ei(x) = ∫_{-∞}^{x} e^{t}/t dt (principal value), which is real for x > 0.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\nThis is a difficult problem that requires careful calculation using Itô calculus and special functions. Let me approach it systematically.\n\n### Setup\n\nThe Itô Langevin equation is:\n$$dp_i(τ) = -\\mu(τ) p_i(τ) dτ + \\sqrt{\\kappa(τ)} dW_i(τ)$$\n\nwhere:\n- $\\mu(τ) = a T^2(τ) = a T_0^2 (\\frac{\\tau_0}{\\tau})^2$\n- $\\kappa(τ) = 2 M T(τ) \\mu(τ) = 2 M a T_0^2 \\frac{T_0}{\\tau} = \\frac{2 M a T_0^3}{\\tau}$\n- $T(τ) = T_0 (\\tau_0/\\tau)^{1/3} = \\frac{T_0}{(\\tau/\\tau_0)^{1/3}}$\n\nThe initial conditions are $p_x(τ_0) = p_0, p_y(τ_0) = 0, p_z(τ_0) = 0$.\n\n### Step 1: Construct the Stochastic Differential Equation\n\nDifferentiating both sides of the Itô equation gives:\n$$dp_i(τ)^2 = -\\frac{2 \\mu(τ)}{1 + \\dot{\\mu}(τ)} \\mu(τ) p_i(τ)^2 dτ^2 + (\\kappa(τ) + \\mu(τ) \\langle p_i \\dot{p}_i \\rangle) dW_i(τ)$$\n\nUsing the fluctuation-dissipation relation $\\kappa(τ) = 2 M T(τ) \\mu(τ)$, and with the "no-fluctuation" (canonical) term $\\langle p_i \\dot{p}_i \\rangle = \\langle -\\mu(τ) p_i^2 dτ \\rangle = -\\mu(τ) \\langle p_i^2 \\rangle dτ$, we get:\n\n$$\\kappa(τ) + \\mu(τ) \\langle p_i \\dot{p}_i \\rangle = 2 M T(τ) \\mu(τ) - \\mu(τ) \\langle p_i^2 \\rangle dτ + \\mu(τ) \\langle p_i^2 \\rangle dτ = 2 M T(τ) \\mu(τ)$$\n\nSo $d(p_i^2/2) = -M \\mu(τ) p_i^2 dτ + \\sqrt{M T(τ) \\mu(τ)} dW_i(τ)$. Let\'s define $q_i(τ) = p_i(τ)/\\sqrt{M}$, so:\n$$dq_i(τ) = -\\sqrt{M} \\frac{dp_i(τ)}{\\sqrt{M}} = -\\sqrt{M} \\left[ -\\mu(τ) q_i(τ) dτ + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\kappa(τ)}} dW_i(τ) \\right] = \\mu(τ) q_i(τ) dτ - \\sqrt{2 M T(τ) \\mu(τ)} dW_i(τ)$$\n\nThis isn\'t quite the right form. Let me try a different approach. Define $u_i(τ) = \\langle p_i(τ) \\rangle$, and consider the system of SDEs:\n\n$$\\{ dp_i(τ) = -\\mu(τ) p_i(τ) dτ + \\sqrt{\\kappa(τ)} dW_i(τ) \\}_{i=1}^3$$\n\nThe mean equations are $dp_i(\\tau)/d\\tau = -μ(τ) u_i(\\tau) + \\text{Var}$, but since we have a non-autonomous system with time-dependent coefficients, we need to be more careful.\n\nActually, let\'s look at the variance first. From the Itô formula:\n$$dp_i(\\tau)^2 = p_i(\\tau)^2 - 2 \\int_0^\\tau \\langle \\dot{p}_i(\\tau\') p_i(\\tau\') \\rangle d\\tau\'$$\n\nwhere $\\langle \\dot{p}_i(\\tau\') p_i(\\tau\') \\rangle = \\langle -\\mu(\\tau\') p_i(\\tau\')^2 d\\tau\' \\rangle = -\\mu(\\tau\') \\langle p_i(\\tau\')^2 \\rangle d\\tau\' + \\text{Correction}$.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me try a different approach using the fact that for large times, we might be able to use asymptotic analysis, but for finite times we need exact solutions.\n\nActually, I realize there\'s a powerful technique I should use. Let\'s consider the following transformation. Define for each particle $i$, a new process $V_i(\\tau) = \\exp(\\int_0^\\tau \\mu(\\tau\') d\\tau\')$. Then,\n\n$$\\frac{dV_i}{d\\tau\'} = V_i(\\tau\') \\mu(\\tau\')$$\n\nSo,\n$$V_i(\\tau) = V_i(\\tau_0) \\exp\\left( \\int_{\\tau_0}^\\tau \\mu(\\tau\') d\\tau\' \\right)$$\n\nNow consider the process $\\tilde{p}_i(\\tau) = v_i(\\tau) p_i(\\tau)$ where $v_i(\\tau) = V_i(\\tau)/V_i(\\tau_0)$. Then,\n$$\\frac{d\\tilde{p}_i}{d\\tau\'} = v_i\'(\\tau\') p_i(\\tau\') + V_i(\\tau\') \\left[ -\\mu(\\tau\') p_i(\\tau\') + \\sqrt{\\kappa(\\tau\')} dW_i(\\tau\') \\right]$$\n\nUsing $v_i\'(\\tau\') = \\mu(\\tau\') v_i(\\tau\')$, we get:\n$$\\frac{d\\tilde{p}_i}{d\\tau\'} = \\mu(\\tau\') v_i(\\tau\') p_i(\\tau\') + V_i(\\tau\') \\left[ -\\mu(\\tau\') p_i(\\tau\') + \\sqrt{\\kappa(\\tau\')} dW_i(\\tau\') \\right] = \\mu(\\tau\') \\langle \\tilde{p}_i(\\tau\') \\rangle + V_i(\\tau\') \\sqrt{\\kappa(\\tau\')} \\frac{1}{V_i(\\tau_0)} dW_i(\\tau\')$$\n\nWith the fluctuation-dissipation relation $\\kappa(\\tau) = 2 M T(\\tau) \\mu(\\tau)$, we have:\n$$V_i(\\tau\') \\sqrt{\\kappa(\\tau\')} = V_i(\\tau_0) v_i(\\tau_0) \\sqrt{2 M T(\\tau\') \\mu(\\tau\')}$$\n\nAnd since $v_i(τ_0) = p_i(τ_0) \\frac{V_i(\\tau_0)}{V_i(\\tau_0)} = p_i(τ_0)$, and with our initial condition $p_x(τ_0) = p_0$, we get:\n\nFor the x-component:\n$$\\frac{d\\tilde{p}_x}{d\\tau\'} = -M \\mu(\\tau\') p_0 + F_x(\\tau\' dW_x(\\tau\'))$$\n\nwhere $F(\\tau\') = M \\sqrt{2 M T(\\tau\') \\mu(\\tau\')}$, so:\n$$df_X(\\tau\') = F(\\tau\') dW_x(\\tau\')$$\n\nThis is a standard setup where we can use moment matching or directly solve the SDE.\n\nActually, let\'s go back. Define $q_i(\\tau) = p_i(\\tau)/\\sqrt{M}$ for each component $i$. Then the SDE becomes:\n\n$$\\frac{dq_i(\\tau)}{d\\tau} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{M}} \\left[ -\\mu(\\tau) q_i(\\tau) d\\tau + \\sqrt{\\kappa(\\tau)} dW_i(\\tau) \\right] = -\\frac{\\mu(\\tau)}{\\sqrt{M}} q_i(\\tau) d\\tau + \\sqrt{\\frac{\\kappa(\\tau)}{M}} dW_i(\\tau)$$\n\nWith $\\kappa(\\tau)/M = 2 T(\\tau) \\mu(\\tau) = 2 T(\\tau) a T^2(\\tau) = 2 a T^3(\\tau)$, so:\n\n$$\\frac{dq_i(\\tau)}{d\\tau} = -a T^2(\\tau) \\sqrt{M \\frac{q_i(\\tau)}{v_i(\\tau_0)}} v_i(\\tau) d\\tau + \\sqrt{2 a T^2(\\tau)} dW_i(\\tau)$$\n\nwhere $v_i(\\tau) = V_i(\\tau)/V_i(\\tau_0) = \\exp(\\int_{\\tau_0}^\\tau \\mu(\\tau\') d\\tau\') = \\exp(a T^2(\\tau_0) \\int_{\\tau_0}^\\tau d\\tau\' / (\\tau/\\tau_0)^{2/3}) = (\\tau/\\tau_0)^{-a T_2(\\tau_0)}$ where $T_2(\\tau_0) = T_0^2 (\\tau_0/\\tau_0)^{1/3} = T_0$.\n\nWait, $T(\\tau) = T_0 (\\tau_0/\\tau)^{1/3}$, so $T^2(\\tau_0) = T_0^2 (\\tau_0) = T_0^2 \\tau_0$. And:\n\n$$\\int_{\\tau_0}^\\tau \\mu(\\tau\') d\\tau\' = a T_0^2 \\int_{\\tau_0}^\\tau d\\tau\' / (\\tau/\\tau_0)^{2/3} = a T_0^2 (\\tau_0)^{2/3} (\\tau^{-1/3} - \\tau_0^{-1/3}) = a T_0^2 (\\tau_0)^{2/3} (\\tau_0/\\tau)^{1/3} ((\\tau/\\tau_0) - 1) = a T_0^3 (\\tau_0^{2/3} - \\tau_0^{5/3}/\\tau) = a T_0^2 (\\tau_0^{2/3} - T_0^{2/3} \\tau^{-1/3})$$\n\nSo $v_i(\\tau) = V_i(\\tau)/V_i(\\tau_0) = \\exp(a T^2(\\tau_0) \\int_{\\tau_0}^\\tau d\\tau\' / (\\tau/\\tau_0)^{2/3}) = (\\tau/\\tau_0)^{-a T_0^2 (\\tau_0^{2/3} - \\tau_0^{5/3}/\\tau) \\cdot c}$ for some constant.\n\nThis seems too messy. Let\'s try a more systematic approach by considering the system of SDEs for the probabilities.\n\n### System of Equations\n\nLet $P(p, \\tau)$ be the probability density at time $\\tau$. From Itô\'s lemma:\n$$dP(p, \\tau)/d\\tau = -\\langle dP \\rangle = \\delta^{(3)}\\langle p(τ) - p \\rangle << dp(τ) $$\n\nSo,\n$$\\langle dp_i(\\tau) \\rangle = \\langle dp_i(\\tau) \\rangle_{prob} = r_i(\\tau) p_i(\\tau)$$\n\nwhere $r_i(\\tau) = \\mu(\\tau)$ is the correlation function (since the covariance is zero for the momentum).\n\nFrom the mean equations:\n$$\\frac{dr_i(\\tau)}{d\\tau} = \\langle \\frac{d^2 p_i(\\tau)}{d\\tau^2} \\rangle = -\\langle \\frac{dp_i(\\tau)}{d\\tau} \\rangle + \\langle dp_i(\\tau) dP(p, \\tau) \\rangle$$\n\nWith the white noise $\\{dP(p, \\tau)\\}$ and the action $\\{dp_i(\\tau)\\}$ orthogonal, and using Itô\'s formula:\n\n$\\langle dp_i(\\tau) dP(p, \\tau) \\rangle = \\langle dp_i(\\tau) \\rangle \\langle p_i(\\tau) \\rangle + \\langle \\dot{p}_i(\\tau) \\rangle \\langle p_i(\\tau) dP(p, \\tau) \\rangle = r_i(\\tau) u_i(\\tau) \\delta(\\delta - p_i)$\n\nSince $\\langle dp_i(\\tau) dP(p, \\tau) \\rangle = 0$, we need to be careful. Actually, with the initial condition $p(\\tau_0) = (p_0, 0, 0)$ and the orthogonality of the noise, we have:\n\nFor $\\tau > \\tau_0$,\n$$\\langle dp_i(\\tau) \\rangle = -\\langle dp_i(\\tau) P(p, \\tau) \\rangle = \\int d^3 p \\langle dp_i(\\tau) \\rangle p_i = r_i(\\tau) u_i(\\tau) \\delta(\\delta - p_i)$$\n\nNow, for the time derivative:\n$$\\frac{dr_i(\\tau)}{d\\tau} = -\\langle \\dot{p}_i(\\tau) \\rangle = \\langle -\\dot{p}_i(\\tau) + m \\dot{v}_i(\\tau) \\rangle = \\langle -\\dot{p}_i(\\tau) - m \\dot{p}_i(\\tau) \\mu(\\tau) \\rangle$$\n\nWait, let\'s use the SDEs more carefully. From Itô\'s lemma:\n$$dp_i(\\tau)^2 = dp_i(\\tau) d\\tau + dp_i(\\tau) \\frac{dp_i(\\tau)}{d\\tau} + \\frac{1}{2} dp_i(\\tau)^2 d\\tau$$\n\nSo,\n$$dp_i(\\tau) d\\tau = dp_i(\\tau)^2 - dp_i(\\tau) \\frac{dp_i(\\tau)}{d\\tau} - \\frac{1}{2} dp_i(\\tau)^2 d\\tau$$\n\nTaking expectations:\n$$\\langle dp_i(\\tau) d\\tau \\rangle = \\langle dp_i(\\tau)^2 \\rangle - \\langle dp_i(\\tau) \\frac{dp_i(\\tau)}{d\\tau} \\rangle$$\n\nWith the correlation structure $\\langle dp_i(\\tau) \\frac{dp_j(\\tau\')}{d\\tau\'} \\rangle = \\lambda_{ij}(\\tau - \\tau\') p_i(\\tau) p_j(\\tau\')$ for $j \\neq i$, and orthogonal for $j = i$ in the linear part (since momentum is conserved linearly), we have:\n\nFor $i = j \\neq x$: $dp_i(\\tau) \\frac{dp_i(\\tau\')}{d\\tau\'} = -\\mu