the forcing $P_\\delta$, the reachability filter for properties satisfying a condition $c$ is determined by how the generic names interact with the iterates of $Q_\\alpha$. Since each $P_\\alpha$ forces that the $N_\\alpha$-iteration of $Q_\\alpha$ is proper, then in the iteration at level $\\kappa + 1$, the elements of the first iterate (for $\\alpha < \\kappa$) are as follows: in $P_{\\alpha}$ for $\\alpha < \\kappa$, there is a condition $q_\\alpha \\in Q_\\alpha[N_{\\alpha,1}]$ such that $Q_\\alpha[N_{\\alpha,1}]$ is proper, so if we take in $P_{\\kappa + 1}$ the condition $q_\\kappa \\cdot q_{\\kappa - 1} \\cdot ... \\cdot q_0$, where $q_i \\in P_i$ are preimages of the generics for the iterated properness, then in $Q_\\kappa = \\slim_{\\alpha < \\kappa} P_\\alpha$, this condition is still $(\\mathcal{M}, Q_\\kappa)$-generic.\n\nFor this to work, we need that the $q_i$ we choose in each $P_i$ are dense and generate proper iterables, which requires that $N_{\\alpha,1}$ is large enough to accommodate the required number of iterates. Since we can have $N_{\\alpha,2}, N_{\\alpha,3}$, etc., each time increasing by some amount, but since $\\delta$ is arbitrary, for large enough $\\theta$, the stationary sets are such that the necessary number of iterates is less than the required $N(\\alpha)$ for each $\\alpha$, so by the pigeonhole principle and homogeneity, we can find a common configuration where all iterates are proper, hence the union condition is also proper.\n\nLet\'s formalize with the $(\\mathcal{M}, Q)$-genericity. Suppose we try to construct a $q \\in P_\\delta[D]$ that is $(\\mathcal{M}, P_\\delta[D])$-generic. By homogeneity, we can take a condition $D$ that is $\\lim$-decreasing with transitive chains, so that we can assume without loss of generality that $D \\cap \\omega = \\emptyset$, $D \\leq \\square R$ for some $R$, and for each $\\alpha$, $D : \\alpha = \\{d_\\alpha \\cdot \\beta : d_\\alpha \\in P_\\alpha, \\beta \\in S_\\alpha\\}$ with $S_\\alpha$ stationary in the right-hand side.\n\nNow, for each repeat in the descent to stationarity, there exists a dense $d_\\alpha \\in P_\\alpha[B_\\alpha]$ with $B_\\alpha \\mapsto Q_\\alpha$, and by the properness assumption in $P_\\alpha$, there is a $q_\\alpha \\in \\sqlim_{\\beta < N_\\alpha} Q_\\alpha[\\phi_\\beta]$ such that $D : \\alpha = q_\\alpha \\cdot B_\\alpha$, so $D$ is $(\\mathcal{M}, Q_\\alpha[\\phi_\\alpha])$-generic in $P_\\alpha$.\n\nNow, consider $q = \\lim q_\\alpha \\in \\sqlim_{\\alpha < \\delta} P_\\alpha \\cdot q_\\alpha$. If we can show that in $P_\\alpha$, $q_\\alpha$ generates a proper iterable, then in $q \\cdot d_\\alpha$, we still have the strong name that $p_\\alpha \\cdot q_\\alpha$ generates a proper iterable, so the generic condition is still true.\n\nSince $P_\\alpha$ forces that for the $N_\\alpha$-iteration, the generics are as above, and by the regularity of $\\delta$ and stationarity of the $N_\\alpha$-iteration, there must exist some $q_\\alpha$ dense and generating, hence the union forces that such a $q$ exists, making the union condition $(\\mathcal{M}, P_\\delta[D])$-generic.\n\nTo make this rigorous, suppose for contradiction that no such $q$ exists, so there is a countable family $(M_\\alpha, p_{\\alpha, A} : \\alpha < \\beta < \\delta)$ as usual. As $|D| \\leq \\omega$, let\'s take $|D| = \\omega$, so $D = \\{d_0 < d_1 < ...\\}$, and for each $\\alpha$, $p_{\\alpha, d} \\in P_\\alpha[D\'_\\alpha]$ where $D\'_\\alpha$ is the $\\alpha$-th iterate of $D$ (i.e., $D\'_\\alpha = \\{d_{\\omega - \\alpha - i} \\cdot c : i < \\omega, c \\in R_{\\alpha}$), and $d_0 < d_1 < ...$. By stationarity of the ordertype $(\\delta, \\omega)$, the merge of the repeatedly constructed families will have stationarity, so there is a stationary set $S \\subseteq (\\omega, \\delta)$ such that for all $\\alpha < \\beta < \\delta$, $dom(p_{\\alpha, \\beta}) \\cap ([ \\alpha, \\beta) \\cap S]^{\\cdot} = \\emptyset$ for some $\\alpha < \\beta$.\n\nTakin